  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nLow confidence forecast remains through Thursday as models\ncontinue to differ on finer details for temps and precip\nchances, especially Wednesday night. Main cold front (much\ncooler and less humid air) is located from Minnesota to central\nNE and is not expected to arrive till Thursday. Much of our \nweather will hinge on pre-frontal troughs, leftover outflow \nboundaries and subtle waves resulting in a lot of challenges on \ncoverage, timing and intensity. \n\nArea of showers and storms has persisted from central IA into\nmuch of WI with only a slow eastward trend. CAMs vary on what\nwill be left of this area as it enters towards 12Z Wed.\nRegardless of what shape it starts, generally expected some\nwidely sct to sct showers and maybe a few storms to persist in a\nsomewhat unstable atmosphere. The marginal severe threat keys \nin on the afternoon and evening hours when pockets of clearing \ncould allow for some increased instability to produce some gusty\nstorms. Overall shear is lacking and lapse rates are rather \nunimpressive so any gusts appear to be isolated at most. Even \nlarger challenges exist where the best instability axis and \nlingering boundaries setup Wednesday night into early Thursday. \nOverall trend has been moreso into central IN resulting in a \ncontinued southward trend to higher pops. Wasn't ready to \ncompletely eliminate pops but did blend with previous forecast \nto nudge down. \n\nEventually the main front will arrive Thursday into Thursday\nnight and usher in an end to any precip and much more\ncomfortable conditions. The battle will then continue between\nthe sprawling upper ridge across the SE states and another\ndeeper trough that will attempt to move in next week. For the\ntime being, no major swings in temperatures or high precip\nchances.",
